"""Original learning goals, practice and assessments; source chapters stay separate."""
CHAPTERS = {
1: dict(goal='Explain why readable, changeable code matters and recognize the chapter’s different perspectives on cleanliness.',ideas=['Precise requirements remain necessary even when tools generate code.','Accumulated disorder makes understanding and changing code more difficult.','Readability, tests, focused responsibilities and care recur across the definitions.','Small ongoing improvements and practice support development; this book represents one school of thought.'],practice='Without looking back, explain why making code easier to read can make the next change easier. Then describe one small improvement that illustrates the Boy Scout Rule. You can answer in your notes; no code editing is required.',check='A useful explanation connects reading to discovering intent, callers and surrounding behavior. A small improvement might clarify a misleading name or remove a little duplication. It should leave the code easier for the next reader. Reading the chapter supplies techniques; developing judgment still takes practice.',questions=[
('A team generates code from a short feature description. Which Chapter 1 concern still applies?',['The required behavior still needs precise specification','Generated code removes the need to clarify requirements','A higher-level language makes ambiguous requirements executable'],0,'The chapter argues that executable behavior requires precision, regardless of the tools or abstraction level.'),
('A team proposes replacing a tangled system. What risk does the redesign story highlight?',['The replacement must match existing behavior while the old system keeps changing','A separate team can ignore changes in the existing product','Starting a new codebase prevents disorder from accumulating'],0,'The replacement races against a moving target and may develop the same maintenance problems. A fresh start alone does not solve the underlying habits.'),
('Why does the chapter connect easier reading with easier writing?',['Changes require understanding the surrounding code','Reading and writing are independent activities','Readability matters only after implementation is complete'],0,'The edit-session example shows developers checking variables, callers and related modules while making changes. Clear surrounding code supports that work.'),
('Which action illustrates the Boy Scout Rule?',['Clarify one misleading name while working in that area','Wait for a complete redesign before improving anything','Add a new feature while leaving every existing issue untouched'],0,'The rule calls for leaving code a little cleaner than it was. Small improvements can accumulate without requiring a complete redesign.'),
('How should the “Schools of Thought” section affect your reading of the book?',['Understand its recommendations while considering other professional perspectives','Treat every recommendation as a universally settled rule','Dismiss the recommendations because some are controversial'],0,'The author presents an experienced school of thought, acknowledges disagreement and encourages learning from other schools. Understanding the rationale matters.')]),
2: dict(goal='Choose names that reveal intent, distinguish concepts and help readers find and discuss code.',ideas=['Communicate meaning, role and relevant units.','Avoid misleading clues, unexplained abbreviations and meaningless differences.','Use consistent vocabulary, useful context and names that can be pronounced and searched.'],practice='Revisit the Minesweeper examples. Explain what gameBoard, FLAGGED and isFlagged tell a reader that the original names did not. Then compare the address examples: when does context help, and when does a product prefix become clutter? Answer in your notes; no code editing is required.',check='gameBoard explains the collection’s role; FLAGGED explains a status value; isFlagged expresses a meaningful question. An Address context clarifies what state means, while a repeated product prefix may obscure a reusable concept. Names should remove guessing without adding irrelevant detail.',questions=[
('A number records days since a file was modified. Which name communicates its meaning and unit?',['daysSinceModification','modificationData','elapsedTime'],0,'daysSinceModification states both the event being measured and the unit. elapsedTime omits the unit and reference event; modificationData does not identify the quantity.'),
('Two parameters in a copy operation are named a1 and a2. Which distinction helps the reader most?',['source and destination','firstData and secondData','data and dataInfo'],0,'source and destination identify the two roles. Ordering or adding Info makes the names different without explaining how they are used.'),
('An account collection might change its underlying representation. Which name avoids promising a particular container type?',['accounts','accountList','accountArray'],0,'accounts communicates the domain concept without claiming a List or array representation. The chapter also cautions that encoding a container type can add unnecessary detail.'),
('A codebase uses fetch, retrieve and get for equivalent operations. What would improve consistency?',['Use one established term for that operation','Keep all three terms to indicate which developer wrote each class','Rename all operations add regardless of their purpose'],0,'A shared term reduces unnecessary translation. Consistency should follow meaning: the same word should not be forced onto operations that do different things.'),
('A variable named state is confusing when read alone. Which change provides useful context?',['Place it in a clearly named Address structure','Prefix every identifier with the product name','Shorten it to s because its type is known'],0,'Address supplies the missing domain context. A product prefix does not explain what state means, and a shorter placeholder requires more mental mapping.')]),
3: dict(goal='Recognize focused functions, consistent abstraction levels and explicit inputs, outputs and effects.',ideas=['A function’s name and steps should express a coherent purpose.','Argument roles, hidden side effects and mixed abstraction levels add work for the reader.','Refine names and structure while preserving behavior; evaluate the author’s Java-oriented recommendations in context.'],practice='Read the before-and-after HTML examples. Explain what became easier to understand. Then inspect the password example: what does the name promise, what else happens, and why could ordering matter? Answer in your notes; no code editing is required.',check='The refined HTML example exposes setup, content and teardown as named steps. checkPassword also initializes a session, so callers must know when that extra change is safe. Explain both intended behavior and hidden effects before choosing a clearer boundary. A shorter function or fewer arguments alone does not prove a better design.',questions=[
('A function mixes a domain operation with formatting newlines and traversing internal paths. What should you examine?',['Whether its steps mix abstraction levels and hide its purpose','Whether every local name has the same length','Whether returning no value would automatically fix its design'],0,'The chapter distinguishes high-level operations from low-level details. A useful extraction should expose a meaningful step rather than merely move lines.'),
('A function named checkPassword also resets the current session. What is the main concern?',['A hidden state change can impose an unexpected ordering requirement','A boolean return value always makes a function incorrect','Its name should be shorter regardless of behavior'],0,'The name suggests a check, while session initialization changes state. Callers can lose data if they do not know when that extra operation is safe.'),
('A function has two arguments representing the coordinates of one point. How does the chapter qualify its preference for fewer arguments?',['Related components can naturally belong together; evaluate their meaning','Every two-argument function must become a zero-argument function','Argument count is the only evidence needed to assess clarity'],0,'The chapter explicitly recognizes a point’s coordinates as a coherent pair. The reader’s work and conceptual roles matter alongside argument count.'),
('A function both sets an attribute and returns whether the operation succeeded. Why can a call in an if statement be confusing?',['The reader may mistake a state-changing command for a query','The function cannot possibly have a meaningful return value','An if statement always violates the one-thing rule'],0,'The chapter’s example illustrates ambiguity between checking an existing value and changing it. Separate or clearly communicate those responsibilities.'),
('You want to improve a complicated function’s structure. Which process matches the chapter’s account?',['Refine names, extract meaningful operations and keep relevant tests passing','Delete branches until the function meets a line-count target','Assume a shorter version preserves behavior without checking'],0,'The author describes revision supported by tests. Size recommendations are part of a design approach, not substitutes for behavior preservation and judgment.')]),
4: dict(goal='Distinguish comments that explain necessary context from comments that duplicate or contradict the code.',ideas=['Improve the code’s expression before using a comment to compensate for unclear intent.','Useful comments can explain rationale, constraints, legal requirements or consequences not evident from the implementation.','Obsolete comments, journal history, commented-out code and unrelated information create maintenance noise.'],practice='Review three comments in a module. Classify each as useful rationale, redundant description or stale information. Improve the surrounding code where it can state the intent itself.',check='Keep a comment explaining a nonobvious integration constraint or dangerous consequence. Remove a comment that repeats i++. Update or remove a statement that contradicts the current implementation. A legal notice should not be deleted simply because it is not executable code.',questions=[
('Which comment is most likely useful?',['A sentence repeating a simple assignment','An obsolete description of a removed branch','An explanation of a nonobvious external constraint'],2,'Comments can supply necessary rationale or constraints that the code cannot express.'),
('A comment says the function returns null, but it now throws on failure. What should happen?',['Update or remove the misleading comment','Keep it as historical documentation','Add more unrelated comments'],0,'Stale comments can mislead readers about the actual contract.'),
('Where does routine change history usually belong?',['Repeated inside every function','Version control or the appropriate tracking system','In the variable names'],1,'The chapter discourages cluttering active code with journal history.'),
('A complex condition needs a comment describing eligibility. What improvement might help?',['Give the condition an intention-revealing helper or variable','Delete the condition without checking behavior','Make every name shorter'],0,'Expressing intent in code can reduce reliance on a compensating comment.'),
('Which is the best reason to remove commented-out code?',['All comments are forbidden','It creates stale ambiguity while history can be retained elsewhere','It always changes runtime speed'],1,'Commented-out code often leaves unclear and obsolete material in active source.')]),
5: dict(goal='Use consistent formatting to make relationships and the reading order visible.',ideas=['Formatting communicates structure; it is part of readable design rather than a cosmetic afterthought.','Use vertical openness for separate concepts and keep closely related code near each other.','Horizontal spacing, indentation and team conventions should make expressions and scope easy to follow.'],practice='Reformat a small module using its team convention. Identify one pair of related operations that is separated unnecessarily and one cluster that mixes unrelated ideas. Explain the intended reading order.',check='The improvement should make relationships easier to see without changing behavior. Use the shared formatter for mechanical consistency, then review placement and ordering separately. Passing formatting checks does not prove the program is correct.',questions=[
('What is the purpose of formatting in this chapter?',['Communicate code structure to readers','Replace behavior tests','Maximize the number of blank lines'],0,'The layout should help readers understand relationships and intent.'),
('When is vertical openness useful?',['Between every token','Between distinct concepts','Only inside string literals'],1,'Whitespace can separate ideas and show structure.'),
('Closely related functions are scattered across a file. What should you evaluate?',['Whether names can be reduced to one letter','Whether all tests can be deleted','Their proximity and the reading order'],2,'The chapter considers vertical distance and ordering important communication tools.'),
('Two programmers prefer different indentation styles. What supports team readability?',['A shared consistent convention','A new style in each function','Avoiding indentation altogether'],0,'Consistent team rules reduce avoidable differences.'),
('A formatter passes. What does that establish?',['Every requirement is correct','Mechanical style consistency, not behavioral correctness','All coupling has been removed'],1,'Formatting checks have a limited scope and do not replace other verification.')]),
6: dict(goal='Choose between objects and data structures deliberately and protect useful abstractions.',ideas=['A useful abstraction hides implementation details; getters and setters alone do not guarantee that.','Objects and data structures favor different kinds of extension: adding types and adding operations have different costs.','Avoid hybrids and long navigation through another object’s internals; understand DTOs and active records in context.'],practice='Review an API that exposes fuel capacity and gallons used. Consider whether callers need those storage details or only the percentage remaining. Write the intended operation and a test for it.',check='A percentage-based operation can hide the storage representation when that is the domain need. It is not automatically better for every consumer: some callers may legitimately need raw measurements. Choose the boundary based on actual operations and change needs.',questions=[
('Do private fields with one getter and setter per field necessarily hide implementation?',['Yes, because every method is an abstraction','No, the same representation can still be exposed','Only if method names are short'],1,'Abstraction concerns what details users must know, not simply the presence of accessor methods.'),
('An object can report percent fuel remaining without exposing stored units. What does this illustrate?',['A domain abstraction','An index lookup','A guarantee of thread safety'],0,'The interface can communicate a meaningful operation while hiding representation.'),
('Which choice should influence objects versus data structures?',['The author’s name','The number of colors in the editor','Which types of extensions and operations the system needs'],2,'The chapter explains the complementary strengths and change costs of these forms.'),
('A method navigates through several objects to reach a low-level field. What concern arises?',['Dependence on another object’s internal structure','A legal comment','The final quiz score'],0,'Transitive navigation can create coupling to representation details.'),
('What is a DTO primarily for?',['Transporting data across a boundary','Hiding all domain behavior in a framework','Executing every business rule'],0,'Data transfer objects provide a straightforward data representation for transfer.')]),
7: dict(goal='Make failure handling understandable without obscuring normal behavior.',ideas=['Use an explicit failure strategy and provide enough context to diagnose errors.','Define exception boundaries around callers’ needs and protect the normal flow from unnecessary detail.','Unexpected null returns and null arguments push ambiguity and checks onto callers; choose an explicit contract.'],practice='Design a lesson-loading contract with a normal result, an absent lesson and a service failure. Explain which is ordinary absence and which is an error. Show how a caller handles each.',check='For example, return an explicitly modeled absence when no lesson exists and raise or return a contextual service failure when retrieval fails. Do not collapse an unavailable service into an empty lesson list. This is an authored application of the chapter, not a universal rule about every language’s error mechanism.',questions=[
('An error only says “failed.” What improvement helps a caller diagnose it?',['Add relevant operation context while preserving the cause','Hide the error completely','Return unrelated sample data'],0,'Useful context helps explain what failed and why.'),
('Several library exception types force every caller to repeat the same handling. What could help?',['Expose every implementation detail permanently','A boundary designed around the caller’s needs','Remove all failure handling'],1,'A wrapper can normalize errors into the meaningful cases callers need.'),
('A service outage is returned as an empty list. What problem can this create?',['It guarantees faster code','It removes all failure states correctly','It confuses unavailable data with a legitimate empty result'],2,'An explicit error strategy should not silently change the meaning of normal results.'),
('Why can unexpected null values make code harder to maintain?',['They spread assumptions and checks through callers','They always prevent allocation','They eliminate ambiguity in every API'],0,'Callers must guess or repeatedly defend against a poorly communicated contract.'),
('What should error handling avoid?',['Relevant context','An explicit boundary','Obscuring the normal operation with duplicated low-level handling'],2,'The chapter emphasizes keeping normal behavior clear and handling failures coherently.')]),
8: dict(goal='Isolate external APIs and learn their actual behavior at well-defined boundaries.',ideas=['A third-party API should not automatically become the internal shape of the entire application.','Learning tests document and verify the behavior you depend on in an external library.','Adapters and interfaces can express your needs even before an external implementation exists.'],practice='Wrap a third-party search client behind the operations your application uses. List two behaviors you need to verify and one library detail you want to keep inside the adapter.',check='Example operations: searchLessons and getLessonById. Verify normal results and failure handling against the library. Keep its option names, exception types or response envelopes inside the adapter. Tests should check your actual dependency, not assume how it works.',questions=[
('Why wrap an external API behind a boundary?',['Make its unrelated details visible to every caller','Limit the application’s dependency on implementation details','Remove every possible future upgrade'],1,'A focused boundary exposes the operations and contract your code actually needs.'),
('What is a learning test?',['A check that explores and records relevant external behavior','A quiz about a publisher','A guarantee about all future library versions'],0,'Learning tests clarify what you depend on and can detect relevant changes.'),
('An external service does not yet exist. What can your code define first?',['Its undocumented implementation details','A random response with no contract','An interface that represents your application’s needs'],2,'The chapter describes defining the needed boundary rather than blocking on an unknown implementation.'),
('What is a useful adapter responsibility?',['Normalize external response and error details into your contract','Copy the entire library API without purpose','Mix unrelated UI state into every library call'],0,'An adapter can contain translation and isolate implementation-specific details.'),
('After an external library upgrade, which checks are especially useful?',['Only a screenshot of the dedication','Learning tests for the behaviors your application depends on','A count of all files in the repository'],1,'These tests target assumptions at the external boundary.')]),
9: dict(goal='Write readable tests that protect behavior and support safe changes.',ideas=['The chapter presents a short test-first cycle and treats test code as code that needs care.','Readable tests expose a clear setup, action and expected result; test-domain helpers can express intent.','F.I.R.S.T. describes fast, independent, repeatable, self-validating and timely tests. Focus each test on a coherent concept.'],practice='Write tests for a lesson lookup covering found, absent and duplicate IDs. Make the intended behavior obvious from the test names and assertions. Check whether one test depends on another’s state.',check='A found test should identify the returned object; an absent test should state the not-found contract; a duplicate test should specify first or last match. Each should arrange its own data. A failing result must be visible without manually reading console output.',questions=[
('What helps a test remain independent?',['Rely on the previous test to create its data','Arrange its own required state','Run only after every other test passes'],1,'Independent tests avoid hidden order dependencies.'),
('A test requires a person to inspect a printed result. Which F.I.R.S.T. quality is missing?',['Self-validating','Pronounceable','Dependency-inverted'],0,'A test should automatically determine whether it passed or failed.'),
('A test is unreadable but passes. Why improve it?',['Tests never need review','Only production naming matters','Readers need to understand the protected behavior when changing code'],2,'Clean tests support maintainability and confidence during refactoring.'),
('What is a useful interpretation of one concept per test?',['Keep assertions centered on a coherent behavior','Ban every test with two assertions regardless of meaning','Put the whole application into one test'],0,'The chapter discusses keeping tests focused without treating assertion counting as the whole design.'),
('Which tests should accompany a boundary-sensitive operation?',['Only its most common input','Normal and relevant boundary/failure cases','Only the formatter check'],1,'Tests should protect the behavior a change could break, including meaningful boundaries.')]),
10: dict(goal='Organize classes around cohesive responsibilities and isolate sources of change.',ideas=['Smallness is about responsibility and reasons to change, not only line count.','Cohesion asks whether methods work with related state for a coherent purpose.','Separate responsibilities and dependencies so a change in one concern does not unnecessarily affect others.'],practice='A class calculates totals, renders HTML and stores receipts. Identify its separate reasons to change. Propose a boundary for one concern without changing the observable workflow.',check='Calculation rules, presentation and persistence can change for different reasons. Extracting one coherent responsibility can reduce coupling, but preserve the workflow and error behavior. Do not split every method into a class without a demonstrated responsibility.',questions=[
('What does the single-responsibility discussion emphasize?',['A class’s coherent responsibility and reasons to change','A universal ten-line limit','One field per class in every system'],0,'The chapter describes responsibility as a better measure than physical size alone.'),
('Methods mostly use unrelated groups of fields. What should you examine?',['The dedication','Whether the class combines separate responsibilities','Whether its name contains uppercase letters'],1,'Low cohesion can indicate several concepts living in one class.'),
('A class computes charges and formats emails. What may motivate separation?',['Both operations necessarily belong in every class','No change is possible','Pricing and presentation can change for different reasons'],2,'Separate concerns can reduce the effect of unrelated changes.'),
('What should a dependency boundary support?',['Substitution and focused verification','More hidden global state','A direct dependency on every implementation'],0,'Isolating details can make a class easier to test and adapt.'),
('Is creating the greatest number of classes the goal?',['Yes, regardless of reader effort','No, seek coherent responsibilities without needless complexity','Only if there are no tests'],1,'The chapter’s design goals do not justify arbitrary fragmentation.')]),
11: dict(goal='Separate system construction from use and make architectural choices based on demonstrated needs.',ideas=['Construction and dependency wiring should not be mixed throughout business operations.','Factories, dependency injection and separation of main can protect these boundaries.','Cross-cutting concerns, architecture tests, appropriate standards and domain language should serve concrete needs rather than speculative complexity.'],practice='Trace how a repository or API client is created in your app. Show where construction ends and normal use begins. Propose a way to provide a test implementation without changing the business operation.',check='Pass the dependency through a constructor or explicit parameter, or use a focused factory at the composition boundary. Business operations can then use the interface rather than instantiate a specific client. The book’s Java proxy and AOP mechanisms are historical implementation examples, not a requirement to add them to a JavaScript app.',questions=[
('Why separate constructing a system from using it?',['To hide all dependencies from reviewers','To keep wiring details from permeating business behavior','To eliminate all design decisions'],1,'Construction and normal operations have different concerns.'),
('What does dependency injection make explicit?',['A supplied dependency instead of constructing it invisibly inside the operation','Every possible future feature','The source book’s publication date'],0,'Providing dependencies can clarify boundaries and enable substitution.'),
('Logging or persistence touches many business operations. What concept may apply?',['A chapter title quiz','A naming convention only','A cross-cutting concern'],2,'Some concerns span multiple parts of the system and need deliberate boundaries.'),
('When should a standard or framework be adopted?',['When it adds demonstrated value for the system','Whenever it is mentioned in a book','Before requirements are understood in every case'],0,'The chapter warns against letting a standard replace judgment about actual needs.'),
('What is a useful role for architecture tests?',['Replace every unit test','Check relevant system behavior and boundaries as design evolves','Prove that every future decision is correct'],1,'Tests can support incremental architecture and guard the behavior you rely on.')]),
12: dict(goal='Apply the simple-design priorities while avoiding duplication and needless entities.',ideas=['Simple design begins with a system that runs all its tests.','Refactoring can reduce duplication and improve expression while preserving behavior.','Minimize classes and methods after correctness and expressiveness; metrics alone do not define a good design.'],practice='Find duplicated behavior in two functions. Explain whether it represents one shared idea or two different policies. Extract only a genuine shared idea and rerun the relevant checks.',check='If both functions implement the same completion rule, a named operation can prevent divergent updates. If they represent different eligibility policies, similar syntax may not justify merging them. The abstraction should express the shared concept rather than make a line count smaller.',questions=[
('Which simple-design priority comes first?',['Run all the tests','Minimize every function count immediately','Use the most frameworks'],0,'The chapter places tested correctness before the later design refinements.'),
('What makes a duplication-removing abstraction useful?',['Its name is short','It represents a genuine shared design idea','It combines different policies without explaining them'],1,'Removing repetition should expose a meaningful concept.'),
('Why can a tiny implementation still be poor design?',['Small code cannot work','Only indentation matters','It may obscure intent or violate behavior'],2,'Smallness is subordinate to correctness and expressiveness.'),
('What should refactoring preserve?',['The intended observable behavior','The exact whitespace in every line','Every confusing identifier'],0,'Refactoring improves internal structure while protecting the contract.'),
('What is an appropriate attitude toward minimizing entities?',['Delete responsibilities until nothing can be tested','Avoid unnecessary structure after meeting the other design goals','Never create a helper'],1,'The priorities guide judgment rather than a blanket prohibition on abstractions.')]),
13: dict(goal='Recognize concurrency risks and separate shared-state coordination from ordinary logic.',ideas=['Concurrency changes execution possibilities and does not automatically improve performance.','Shared mutable data and compound operations need an explicit coordination strategy.','Keep critical sections focused, understand library guarantees, plan shutdown and treat intermittent failures as evidence to investigate.'],practice='Model two workers incrementing a shared count. Explain a possible lost update and propose an ownership, copy or synchronization strategy. State what a test can and cannot establish.',check='Both workers can read the same old value, compute the same increment and overwrite one another. A suitable atomic or synchronized operation, or eliminating shared mutation, can address that contract. Repeated passing runs alone do not prove that every interleaving is safe. Java threads and browser async operations are different mechanisms; do not equate them blindly.',questions=[
('Two workers read the same count and both write count + 1. What may happen?',['A lost update','A guarantee of two increments','No possible race'],0,'Reading, computing and writing form a compound operation with interleaving risks.'),
('Individual methods are synchronized. Does a multi-method sequence automatically become atomic?',['Yes in every case','No, the whole sequence needs its own contract and protection','Only its names matter'],1,'Dependencies across separately protected methods can still produce races.'),
('Threaded tests passed repeatedly. What can you conclude?',['Every possible interleaving is proved safe','Testing is useless','The tested runs passed; other schedules can still expose faults'],2,'Concurrency testing supplies evidence but cannot establish all possible schedules from repetition alone.'),
('Which approach can reduce shared-state risks?',['Clear ownership or independent copies of data','A larger critical section around the entire application in every case','Ignoring intermittent failures'],0,'The chapter discusses limiting shared data and keeping work independent.'),
('Why plan shutdown explicitly?',['Waiting workers and outstanding work need coordinated termination','Shutdown cannot affect behavior','All libraries guarantee correct shutdown automatically'],0,'Correct shutdown has its own coordination and lifecycle risks.')]),
14: dict(goal='Follow the Args case study as a sequence of verified design decisions rather than memorizing Java listings.',ideas=['A working first implementation can become difficult to extend as responsibilities accumulate.','Small transformations, tests and focused argument representations support successive refinement.','Compare each rough and refined version by its contracts, duplication, coupling and clarity; listings are stages of one case study.'],practice='Trace how boolean, integer and string arguments are handled in the rough and refined Args designs. Choose one transformation and explain the problem, the preserved behavior and the check needed before proceeding.',check='A useful explanation might show how an ArgumentMarshaler separates the behavior for a type from the parser’s traversal. Verify accepted options, missing values and conversion errors. The exact implementation is Java-based; the transferable skill is explaining and checking each change, not copying class names into another language.',questions=[
('What is the main learning goal of successive refinement?',['Explain small verified transformations from a working design to a clearer one','Memorize every class name in the listing','Rewrite without understanding existing behavior'],0,'The case study demonstrates the decisions and checks in an incremental improvement process.'),
('The parser gains another argument type. What concern should a reviewer examine?',['Whether the type starts with the same letter','Whether handling responsibilities and repeated logic are becoming tangled','The chapter’s publisher'],1,'Growing requirements can expose weak responsibility boundaries.'),
('What must be checked after changing argument handling?',['Only the indentation','Only the file name','Valid options, missing values and relevant conversion errors'],2,'The parser’s externally meaningful success and failure behavior must remain correct.'),
('A long listing is split across lesson slides. How should you read it?',['As consecutive portions of the same labeled version','As unrelated runnable programs','As evidence that the missing parts do not matter'],0,'The course labels listing continuations so readers preserve the case-study sequence.'),
('Which evidence best justifies an extraction in the case study?',['The number of classes increases','A coherent responsibility becomes clearer while tested behavior is retained','The new name is more fashionable'],1,'A useful transformation addresses a concrete problem without hiding a contract change.')]),
17: dict(goal='Use smells and heuristics to investigate concrete code problems rather than apply slogans mechanically.',ideas=['Comments, environment, functions, general design, Java, names and tests provide different review lenses.','A smell is a prompt to investigate responsibilities, behavior, dependencies and context.','Use explanatory variables, explicit boundaries, consistent conventions and focused tests where they address an observed problem. Java-specific advice stays distinct from general guidance.'],practice='Review one module using three heuristics from different categories. For each, record the observation, consequence, proposed change and check. Include a case where a guideline does not justify a change.',check='Example: an unexplained constant suggests a named domain concept; a hidden sequencing requirement suggests making the dependency explicit; an untested boundary suggests a focused test. A similar-looking pair of functions may encode different policies, so do not merge them mechanically. Treat the author’s strong recommendations as guidance to evaluate in context.',questions=[
('What is the best use of a code smell?',['A prompt to investigate a concrete problem in context','An automatic proof that code must be rewritten','A score based only on appearance'],0,'Heuristics guide reasoning; they do not remove the need to understand the code.'),
('What can replace an unexplained constant when it represents a domain rule?',['Another unexplained number','A meaningful named constant','A comment containing unrelated history'],1,'A name can make the meaning and role of the value explicit.'),
('A function only works when another operation was called first, but the API hides that dependency. What should you examine?',['The cover image','Only its length','A hidden temporal coupling'],2,'An implicit order requirement can surprise callers and should be made understandable.'),
('Which testing heuristic directly addresses input limits?',['Test boundary conditions','Only run happy paths','Skip every trivial-looking case'],0,'The chapter explicitly calls for care at boundaries.'),
('How should Java-specific import advice be used in a TypeScript project?',['Apply it literally to every TypeScript import','Distinguish language-specific advice from transferable principles','Delete all imports'],1,'The Java category is preserved as source material without pretending its exact mechanisms apply to every language.')]),
}

